    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Chester Sage and Brayton B. founded the community of Sageville in 1833.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sageville_iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Sageville was named in honor of Chester Sage and grew from a milling venture that processed local timber.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sageville_iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sageville developed around milling activities along the Little Maquoketa River during the early nineteenth century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sageville_iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Sageville's origins are tied to the Black Hawk Purchase of 1833, which opened millions of acres to non-Native American pioneers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sageville_iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Sageville lies within the Paleozoic Plateau, a rugged, unglaciated region historically known as part of the Driftless Area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sageville_iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Early arrivals in Dubuque County established mining camps and basic industries, leading to the creation of Sageville in Peru Township.",
